A former college football player and wrestler who now runs a concussion research group argues that technology is about to improve four things: impact indicators, sideline assessment, return-to-play testing and "hit counts." He says concussions are badly under-diagnosed, return to play is poorly timed, and subconcussive blows may also damage the brain. He does rough math to show that perfect detection and cautious return would sideline a fifth of players, so the sports themselves would have to change, starting with less contact in practice. He also discusses helmets, informed consent, and genetics. He discloses ties to a test and a company.

KC: - Technology is improving assessment, removal from play, return to play and hit counts [00:30]. - Concussions do not show on standard imaging; diagnosis relies on signs and symptoms [05:04]. - Diagnosed concussions run about 5% a year in contact sports, but anonymous athlete surveys give numbers more like 50% [06:04]. - In a hockey study, putting a doctor in the stands to pull players liberally found seven times as many concussions: 5% versus 35% [06:34], [07:04]. - Impact thresholds are a problem: risk is an S-curve, no known concussion at 10 G, and certain concussion at 300 G, so any red-light threshold is arbitrary [08:04], [08:35]. - The current 20-minute sideline evaluation has not been validated and its parts cannot decide return to play alone [09:36]. - Substitution rules in rugby and soccer make it hard to remove and evaluate players, and a "brain bin" 20-minute window has been p
LIM: - The speaker discloses a consultant role and a financial tie to a test's owner [00:30]. - The 21% and 4% figures are his back-of-envelope math using assumed recovery times, with "the science is iffy" on the 10-day figure [12:12]. - Brain-bank findings are a self-selected, biased sample, as he says [15:15]. - Header study is unpublished; a threshold for safe hit counts is unknown [18:51]. - Caption problems: names and organizations garbled (sports legacy institute, "SC stat too" likely SCAT2, "K
