A moderated panel of NFL front-office and analytics people and a high school coach discusses where analytics help football and where they stop. The panel covers how much information a quarterback can carry to the line, what the combine is still good for, why speed numbers miss reaction and vision, and why fourth-down and other in-game decisions are the easiest gains. A recurring theme is that a small edge in the odds is hard to sell to coaches and owners whose jobs depend on short-term results. The panelists also say communication matters more than data volume, and that player performance, hydration and injury prevention are underused areas.

KC: - A quarterback should be given a distilled set of situational tendencies, because too much analytics makes players think instead of react [04:08]. - The NFL does not allow computers or technology in the coach's booth, so context has to be carried by people [05:38]. - Coach decision-making in games (such as fourth-down choices) is described as low-hanging fruit for analytics [06:39]. - It is not currently possible to measure how a quarterback decides under duress, and the panel says the physical punishment a quarterback takes cannot be replicated in training [08:45], [12:19]. - One panelist says clutch performance exists but is mostly a mirage, and that players are already selected for handling pressure [13:53]. - The combine is still useful for medical checks, interviews, and a standardized comparison of players, and physical traits matter more in later rounds [17:58]. - Speed and playi
