Two people from the league office's analytics and data products group demonstrate a platform that all 32 teams can use, built about three years ago. The talk opens with a short league network segment on two quarterbacks, then walks through filterable reports (passer rating, personnel groupings, success rate, a scorecard, player similarity, combine similarity, passing charts) linked to video. The stated lesson is that making data easy to use matters most. The talk is a product demonstration; it does not test whether the reports improve decisions.

KC: - The platform was built so the business can get information directly, without depending on other departments [06:08]. - The system holds every NFL stat; nothing was selected out [23:16]. - Personnel groupings are known for every play, so stats can be filtered by grouping, defense, down, quarter and others [06:39] [09:43]. - The success definition is configurable by the user [09:43]. - Video is integrated with the analytics so filtered plays can be watched [10:46] [11:17]. - A scorecard of keys to winning is set up ahead of time, with green, red and yellow by game [11:48]. - Player similarity compares players on a standardized year in the league, so players from different entry years line up [12:54]. - Combine similarity matches players on measurements within a percentage range [15:28] [15:59]. - Passing charts use zones measured in air yards and can be color-customized by the team [16:3
LIM: - No evidence is shown that the reports improve decisions or that similar early years predict later years. - Many demonstrated splits are small (14 plays, one pass, seven targets). - The platform was shown by its builders; its adoption is described only loosely [20:44] [24:18]. - Names in the caption: "Chris tank", "Jeremy Troy", "Marce Jones", "MJD" and others are caption spellings. - The demonstration had technical delays [04:22] and parts were skipped [08:43]. - The opening clip's quarterback
