A hockey front-office member reflects on bringing analytics into a junior team and then an NHL club. He argues analytics did not so much build his roster as teach him that his eyes and mind mislead him. He walks through recency bias, sample-size bias and a bias toward players who "keep it simple", and says the real lesson is the difference between knowing a fact and valuing it in decisions. He also stresses that analytics takes time and needs organization-wide buy-in, including the coach. The evidence is his own experience, mostly one team and one coaching change.

KC: - Collecting data is different from valuing and using it in a team process. [05:16] - After a coaching change on the same roster, the team's shot share rose. [06:47] - Buy-in across the organization (president, general manager, coach, scouts) gives more success than one person pushing analytics. [07:18] - Analytics served him as a teacher more than as a builder. [07:49] - "Eyeballs business" thinking is wrong: eyes and mind can fool you if you allow it. [08:20] - Recency bias: one scouted game can override hundreds of games and many data points. [09:51] - A World Juniors appearance inflated a draft list. [10:21] - A hot training camp can displace a player who has proven himself over years, including losing him on waivers. [10:51] - Simplicity bias: scouts favor safe players who "keep it simple", which he distrusts. [11:51] [12:22] - A playoff sweep can be driven by an unsustainably low s
