Glenn Fleisig of the American Sports Medicine Institute explains the rise of ulnar collateral ligament (Tommy John) surgery in baseball and what his group's data say about it. He covers the stress on the elbow, how often pitchers return after surgery, risk factors, and prevention. His headline finding: too much pitching, and especially pitching while fatigued, is the strongest risk factor he has found [33:31]. Curveballs were not shown to be more dangerous than fastballs, though he says they were not proven safe [52:31]. He closes with MLB and youth prevention efforts (PitchSmart) and wearable and camera biomechanics tools, some from a company he advises [41:13].

KC: - Professional UCL surgeries were steady for about 20 years, then climbed, so the rise is not a one-year fluke [07:42]. - About 16 percent of professional pitchers have had the surgery, with a higher share among major leaguers that he attributes to more years pitching [08:13], [09:14]. - The ligament has to resist most of the elbow stress in the cocked position, and cadaver work showed the ligament alone cannot take that load, so muscle and other tissue share it [16:28], [17:00]. - The surgery does not improve velocity or performance; he believes the two studies that found performance down toward age-expected levels [21:39], [22:10]. - Return to the majors takes about a year and a half in published studies, about 12 months in his group's data [22:40], [24:15]. - In a spring training comparison, pitchers who had returned from surgery matched never-injured pitchers on flexibility and mecha
