A Wired editor and author of a sports-science book argues that elite results turn on tiny margins and that the high-performance world offers lessons for any field. He covers variation in a single athlete's performance, the "aggregation of marginal gains" idea from British cycling, how different bodies and genes make athletes individual, measurement effects, consumer and team technology, and sleep. He closes by saying the only sustainable advantage is learning faster than the competition. Much of the evidence is illustration and anecdote, and some figures are the speaker's retelling of other people's work.

KC: - Margins at the elite level are tiny, so even a blowout can sit inside normal day-to-day variation. [05:09] [05:40] - Variation is about one percent in short events and larger in long and technical events. [06:11] [06:41] - British cycling improved by finding many small gains instead of one large one. [09:15] [09:45] - Small items (own pillows, hand washing, beet juice, wiping tires, heated pants) were used for small edges. [11:46] [12:16] [14:19] [14:50] - Elite athletes are physically individual, and bodies match their sports. [16:51] [19:26] - Early specialization is argued against, because sampling sports helps find the right fit. [19:56] - Trainability varies by genetics, and few people combine a high baseline with a strong training response. [21:58] [23:29] - Genes set the size of the "bucket" and environment decides how much is poured in. [24:30] - Being measured may change perfo
