A 2015 panel of NCAA, university, law and NBA voices debates how college sports should change as money, lawsuits and the Power 5 split reshape the amateur model. Panelists discuss whether athletes are employees under labor law, an antitrust challenge to scholarship caps, Title IX limits on paying revenue-sport athletes, the NBA age rule, and the role of agents. Most speakers favor gradual recalibration over abolition, with more weight on academics and athlete health and welfare. No analytics or data models are shown. The value for a performance staff is the policy context that shapes eligibility, time demands and what athlete data collection could mean legally.

KC: - Jim Phillips (Northwestern) says campus athletics exists to teach skills such as teamwork that a classroom does not, and that academics must be the base of any reform. [03:37] - Oliver Luck says he joined the NCAA because he thinks the college model is unique in the world and worth saving while reforming what is broken. [06:11] - Rod Thorne (NBA, caption spelling) says the league's concern is player health, including very dense schedules, and that the injury level is no higher than in recent years. [08:15] - Mike mccan (caption spelling) explains that if football players are declared employees, a separate vote is still needed to unionize, and a ruling could let athletes at other private schools rely on it. [10:17] [10:47] - Mike notes the employee question is tricky because only some athletes (football) might unionize, leaving others unclear. [11:17] - Jim lists recent reforms: unlimit
LIM: - No data, analytics or models were presented; this is opinion and legal commentary. - Speakers represent the NCAA, a university, the NBA and a law school; each speaks from an institutional position. - Outcomes were pending in 2015 (labor board decision, appeal of the antitrust case). - Captions do not mark speaker changes; some attributions above are by context. Several names, case names ("oanon", "Kesler") and figures are garbled. - Claim that coaches are the highest paid public employee in 40
