The speaker argues that sports data scientists need subject matter knowledge, including basic physiology, because athletes differ by sport and by position, and so should the way they are measured. She compares NFL and NBA combine results by position, questions whether single tests (the bench press especially) suit every position, and presents a measurement model with physical, physiological, psychological, behavioral and environmental variables. She briefly describes a tennis pilot on narcissism and calls for individualized "precision athleticism" using wearables, borrowed from precision medicine. The talk is a broad survey drawn from her new book, with few numbers and no validation data.

KC: - Data scientists in sport should be able to discuss their sport as a fan would, not only the numbers [02:05]. - Muscle fiber type is partly genetic and partly trained; type one fibers resist fatigue but give less power and force, type two the reverse [04:37]. - The three energy systems are phosphagen, glycogen and oxidative; a maximal sprint relies on the first only briefly before speed drops [06:42, 07:14]. - Between sports, and between positions within a sport, athletes should be assessed and trained differently [10:16]. - NFL combine results differ by position, shown with a simple ANOVA on public data [13:52]. - The bench press may not suit some positions; she asked whether a quarterback should be tested on it at all and said she was only posing questions [13:52]. - Height is not the best predictor in basketball; wingspan and hand size are better [17:00]. - Psychological variables ar
LIM: - The tennis pilot gave no sample size, results or validation [20:35 to 22:05]. - Combine position comparisons are not tied to game performance or to injury. - Self-report measures are acknowledged as biased [20:05]; the implicit task was shown only as a similar example. - The cryotherapy, stem cell and gene editing remarks are loose and not evidenced [24:39, 26:43]. - Claim that the injury risk of a quarterback tested on the bench press is raised is explicitly not asserted [13:52]. - Book promo
