Phil Wagner, founder of Sparta Science, argues that a standardized vertical jump on a portable force plate can flag injury risk and physical readiness, including upper body injuries in baseball pitchers. He models the approach on medicine: clinical expertise, athlete input and evidence, plus a standardized assessment. The jump is scored on three phases (load, explode, drive) into a "movement signature" with thresholds, and software turns imbalances into a prioritized exercise plan. Injury history, sport load and sleep sit on a dashboard. The talk is a vendor presentation with case studies and claimed results, but no data tables appear in the captions. The Q&A covers balance and landing tests, buy-in, football versus baseball targets and youth use.

KC: - A jump can predict an injury, specifically an upper body injury. [01:05] - Building prediction models generally takes 3 to 5 years of data, and Sparta has about seven years across MLB, NFL, NCAA and several countries. [03:36] [04:07] - The jump was chosen because other tests (sprinting, agility, throwing, hitting, single-leg jumps) are less reliable. [09:05] - The jump is split into load (standing to squat), explode (how quickly the athlete comes out of the bottom) and drive (how well the movement is prolonged); each is tied to success in different positions or sports. [09:05] [09:36] - The three scores form a "movement signature," expressed as a t-score where 50 is average. [10:06] [10:39] - Plates and vertical jumps are not new; what is new is thresholds for what is meaningful. [10:39] - In one pitcher case, a particular uneven pattern (a "healthy imbalance") is associated with groun
LIM: - No data, sample sizes, false positive rates or confidence intervals appear in the captions. "Predict" is used without saying whether prospective or retrospective. - The 99 injuries and savings figures are garbled or inconsistent. - The publication on drive and elbow injury is not named. [12:43] - Third-party validation (Stanford, BYU) is stated without results. [11:10] - The 10 to 20% yearly reduction has no stated comparison group. [17:24] - The 14-day claim is stated as known with no evidenc
