Two industrial and organizational psychologists describe a one-hour psychological assessment given to every player at the NFL combine since 2013. It measures cognitive ability and personality traits tied to football, and is meant as an addition to physical and film evidence. A three-year validation study compared combine scores with coach ratings of players after at least a year, plus penalties and injuries. Correlations are modest (about 0.22 to 0.35) but the speakers show that top and bottom scorers are rated very differently. The second half describes player-type clusters, a bust-rate comparison using a public value metric, and planned longitudinal modeling of second contracts.

KC: - The goal was to add a standardized psychological measure alongside combine physical tests, not to replace them [04:13], [06:17]. - A league study with team executives produced 16 factors, with intelligence and personality as the two key areas [05:46]. - Intelligence items cover learning, thinking quickly and avoiding mental errors; personality items cover leadership, teamwork, self-control, dependability, preparedness and team fit [06:50]. - The test has run at the combine since 2013 with full participation, and the data were also turned into a coaching report [07:20], [07:51]. - The validation compared pre-draft scores with coach ratings after the players had been on a team for at least a year [09:54]. - The speaker calls correlations of this size good for behavioral science and says they are uncorrected [10:24]. - High, middle and low test scores matched coach rating bands; coaches w
