===== 8XrPoEKFpvA
A presentation from a company that combines athlete tracking data with coaching video. The main speaker, a former college football graduate assistant, argues that data only matters if coaches understand it and believe it. He uses a personal story about missing bowl-game stats to show that analysts must know the limits of their data. He describes observed versus laboratory metrics, the value of consistent third-party data, and the need to validate new tracked metrics against film. He says staff should set thresholds, and analysts should earn trust by checking current decisions. The talk is a vendor presentation.

KC: - The company's data pairs with coaching video, since coaches work in video. [02:06] - Analysts must know the boundaries of their data; a missed bowl-game stat in a report cost the speaker and a colleague credibility with the head coach. [05:41] - Metrics have long come in two kinds: observed (video, practice) and laboratory. [06:42] - Some fields are empirical (down, distance) and others subjective (formation, personnel), and verbiage differs by staff. [08:14] - Third-party data gives consistent definitions across teams. [09:46] - A defined metric such as burn defender can save coaches large amounts of review time, at the cost of some false positives. [10:18] - Coaches grew willing to use third-party data after comparing it with their own for about a year. [10:49] - Staff buy-in decides whether findings are used. [12:23] - Metrics must be accurate and precise, and must help win a game. 
LIM: - The talk is a vendor presentation; claims about the value of combining video and wearable data are the speaker's commercial view. - No data, sample sizes or results were shown for any validation. - Captions are very garbled in several passages (speed thresholds, false-positive counts, statistics). - Names in the story may be misspelled. Adam "gay" in the captions was named as a former student and later a head coach of the Dolphins (caption spelling). - Smart-ball and heart-rate examples are de
